#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060512} :activity/id #uuid "17339bb9-1659-5064-a793-2d84ac6e8242" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060891 :tutorial/content "The following two identities are known as the **sum and difference formulas for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x\\,+\\,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\,-\\,\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x\\,-\\,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\,+\\,\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson.\n\nNotice that the signs are somewhat counterintuitive. The sum formula for cosine contains a minus sign $({\\color{red}{-}}),$ while the difference formula contains a plus sign $({\\color{blue}{+}}).$ \n\nThese identities can help calculate the cosine of an angle that can be expressed as the sum or difference of two special angles.\n\nFor example, we can use the sum formula to find the exact value of $\\cos(105^\\circ).$ First, we write\n\n$$\n105^\\circ = {\\color{blue}45^\\circ} + {\\color{red}60^\\circ}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{105}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}60-\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}60 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9114a20-3220-5a44-b0ed-4e405d4317a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2563 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2afe3115-494d-5055-8e96-efb5e53aeb5e" :step/math-academy-id 11766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059496 :question/id #uuid "1d8679d3-8922-5471-811f-60c8c41c853a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3522" :question/problem "Express $\\cos\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749553} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749551}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "53346d2f-5b1b-51d4-a6bb-f60bd1e5c54e" :step/math-academy-id 11767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059666 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059497 :question/id #uuid "bdb79801-7c8f-5b39-8730-85dcc3d9cdd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3523" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\cos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x - y) = \\cos{x}\\cos{y} + \\sin{x}\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715295} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186714827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715397}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "a8c43958-8da7-5043-96a5-0deaa552b575" :step/math-academy-id 11772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059628 :question/id #uuid "b643e820-52c3-5c08-a099-aecbed7b2b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9991" :question/problem "Express $\\cos(x - 60^\\circ)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos{u}\\cos{v} + \\sin{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = 60^\\circ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715675} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715695}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "8279c645-73d9-562a-a923-1077bbc0cf0e" :step/math-academy-id 37636 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060864 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nConsider two points $P$ and $Q$ on the unit circle whose central angles have measures $A$ and $B,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5ed2cf9e528e7ec6762875f3dfaf211aba656e6b39025109315539a7dd4b013a.png)\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ lie on the unit circle, their coordinates are\n\n$$\nP(\\cos A, \\sin A), \\qquad Q(\\cos B, \\sin B).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance $PQ^2$ between $P$ and $Q$ can be found using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P{Q}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{cos}A-\\operatorname{cos}B)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}A-\\operatorname{sin}B)}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}A-2\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}B+{cos}^{2}B+{sin}^{2}A-2\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}B+{sin}^{2}B \\\\  & =({cos}^{2}A+{sin}^{2}A)+({cos}^{2}B+{sin}^{2}B)-2(\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}B+\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\cos^2 B + \\sin^2 B = \\cos^2 A + \\sin^2 A = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $A$ and $B$ by the Pythagorean identity, our expression for $PQ^2$ simplifies as\n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 1+1 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B)\n$$\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 2 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B).\n$$\n\nWe can also calculate $PQ^2$ by applying the law of cosines to $\\triangle POQ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P{Q}^{2}={1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(A-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 2 - 2\\cos(A-B).\n$$\n\nEquating the two expressions for $PQ^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n2 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B) = 2 - 2\\cos(A-B)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "98298911-e456-5b6c-92f0-315fe2bfca4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13282 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Difference Formula"} :step/id #uuid "00e554fc-e6f5-5f65-bed6-576c4f6d26f7" :step/math-academy-id 461993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060865 :tutorial/content "We have shown how to derive the difference formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B.\n$$\n\nWe can derive the sum formula for cosine by simply replacing $B$ with $-B,$ and using the evenness/oddness identities for cosine and sine, respectively:\n\n$$\n\\cos(-B) = \\cos B, \\qquad \\sin(-B) = -\\sin B\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into our difference formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(A-(-B))=\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}(-B)+\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}(-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\cos(A+B) = \\cos A\\cos B - \\sin A\\sin B.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "67134b4c-9f78-59b0-b0e7-42049c393c9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13283 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Sum Formula"} :step/id #uuid "5e38ce17-dfbd-58ac-95c8-c7020d6e1b3c" :step/math-academy-id 461994}] :activity/title "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Cosine" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059948}]}
